he quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant \\( \\Delta \\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = (-60)^2 - 4(-16)(54)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3600 + 3456\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 7056\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the square root of the discriminant:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{7056} = 84\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( b \\), \\( \\sqrt{\\Delta} \\), and \\( a \\) back into the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-(-60) \\pm 84}{2(-16)}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{60 \\pm 84}{-32}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two potential solutions for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[\nt_1 = \\frac{60 + 84}{-32} = \\frac{144}{-32} = -4.5\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt_2 = \\frac{60 - 84}{-32} = \\frac{-24}{-32} = 0.75\n\\]\n\nSince time \\( t \\) cannot be negative, we discard \\( t_1 = -4.5 \\). Therefore, the only valid solution is:\n\n\\[\nt = 0.75\n\\]\n\nThus, the ball hits the ground in \\(\\boxed{0.75}\\) seconds."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0.75}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:353"}
